      "writtenOn": "2023-01-13T22:13:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we get a trusty interrupt right before this, trusty_work.signaled will be set (and the priority should already be at the nop_nice_value or above). I think the code below will not necessarily choose the correct priority in this case. It should be treated the same as a dequeued nop request in that it should make sure the priority is at least as high as nop_nice_value. This needs to be compared with the priority request from trusty though, not with the current thread priority which could still be running at an elevated priority from a previous request. It would be good to add some comments here.",
      "revId": "1f5770fc0d450aac797ad4635991a354061d7d47",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dd6818bc_f1cfe9d1",
        "filename": "drivers/trusty/trusty.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 804,
      "author": {
        "id": 1964575
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-20T21:38:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we get an interrupt right before this (and after dequeue_nop), we would handle it the next go around with the queued nop or tw-\u003esignaled. Is this acceptable? Otherwise we will need to change where tw-\u003esignaled is handled to here and return it so one level up the call stack will call back into Trusty if the interrupt happened after dequeue_nop.\nI\u0027ve changed the priority logic to always read Trusty request and then override with nop_nice_value if we\u0027ve been signaled or there\u0027s a nop queued.",
      "parentUuid": "41a11845_e402986b",
      "revId": "1f5770fc0d450aac797ad4635991a354061d7d47",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "78ff84fc_5f81ed81",
        "filename": "drivers/trusty/trusty.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 804,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-20T22:18:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think you need to change anything other than also read tw-\u003esignaled  here. Letting the priority potentially get lowered to low here and waiting until the next time linux decides to schedule this thread before setting it back to a high priority can create a glitch that we should easily be able to avoid.",
      "parentUuid": "dd6818bc_f1cfe9d1",
      "revId": "1f5770fc0d450aac797ad4635991a354061d7d47",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "de3b3fa7_0f2689f7",
        "filename": "drivers/trusty/trusty.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 858,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-13T22:13:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why are you passing false here? Also it might be simpler to always call trusty_adjust_nice_nopreempt instead of only when trusty gets preempted. If trusty for instance requests a low priority, gets preempted, runs at the low priority, then goes idle then sees another nop_request from dequeue_nop, it seems it will call into trusty to process that nop request while still running at a low priority. Also, if trusty ran at a higher priority than nop_nice_value when it last went idle, the next nop request will also run at that high priority. Switching the thread back to nop_nice_value everytime it goes back to the blocking state might be better.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 858,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 858,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "1f5770fc0d450aac797ad4635991a354061d7d47",
